And slowly, then, we had to start wearing the stars on the front jackets. Again, as kids, you know, it was a lot, so we would throw the jacket over our shirt to hide it sometimes, which was, of course, against the law. But the-- the-- the-- the-- the horrible thing was, I'm sure, if--on-- on my parents, who-- who saw what was happening and what was going on.

And so the noose was starting to tighten evermore until it came to October '41, when the first transports were being prepared. This was before Terezín or Theresienstadt, was ever opened, before Auschwitz or anything. And the Germans decided to send 5,000-- five transports of 1,000 each of Jews to Poland. Initially they wanted the wealthier ones to go so that they could take their apartments for their own use, and their furniture.

And we got a notice on the third transport, together in-- in one of the assembly points. We were told that we could take along 50 pounds, two suitcases plus a little bit of hand luggage, and gather there, I think, on the 23rd or 21st of October, 1941, and then we'll be shipped further out for labor.

The last night, I remember my mother crying, my father being greatly disturbed, our cousin with her Gentile husband still visiting us. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] We kids, particularly my brother, and even myself, felt that-- you know, were encouraging our parents, told them that we were young, but we were strong, and we were no little kids, and that we'll make it and we shouldn't worry.

My brother, who was then into a little bit of athletics, wanted to pack his weights, which, of course, was quickly turned down by my parents because there were more valuable things to-- to put in within the 50 pounds than five or 10 pounds of weights.

And we packed our suitcases, and we were, I guess, taken by taxi or what of my--through-- through our relatives to the assembly point. There, for two or three days, we slept on mattresses on the floor in this huge hall, 1,000 people, each one assigned exactly one mattress space. And this was devastating, as I say, to my parents. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] To us, again, it looked more like camping than anything else.

When we were assembled to march out, some people did not obey the law of 50 pounds and had more luggage. Well, the German captain of the SS, captain in charge, said that there is more luggage than there should be, and therefore he's going to take from every family one piece of luggage, which, again, my father then gave up his, where he had his shaving kit and a few other-- everything carefully prepared. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

And we boarded the train. At that time, this was still passenger cars, and I believe it was not only a third class hardwood, but I think second class, somewhat better-- better rail cars where we were seated. We occupied them. The luggage was in the luggage-- in the baggage cars, and the train took off. We didn't know-- nobody knew-- what the destination was. We could see through the blackened out windows, guarded by the German soldiers, that we were moving east. I remember that we recognized, or somebody recognized that we were passing through Breslau, Vratislav.

And finally, on a wintry, cold, dark night, we arrived in Lódz, at the Lódz ghetto. The rail line was just on the edge of the ghetto. It was dark. It was evening. It was snowing. When we-- when we got out, the Germans with the dogs and-- and the shouting and the screaming drove us all out. And then, under the guidanceship of the ghetto Jewish police, we marched. And the luggage was being hand-- in hand carts, carried on to an assembly point.

We were housed in a school which was converted. Bunks, two or three high, housed the 1,000 people there. And after a day or two, we settled down, were given some soup. Nobody was much interested in eating. And Father started exploring with us boys where we were and what we did.

And I still remember that within a week or two, we went to visit the Jewish cemetery of [NON-ENGLISH], I think the name was, of the ghetto. Well, by then, already people were dying by the dozens daily. There were some 250,000 people jammed into this small area of the city, and one funeral after another, just carrying them in on a--on a-- some-- some wooden things, and the bodies dumped into individual graves that were dug.

And Father always, in the past-- when we've came into some town, a new town, whether it was in Czechoslovakia or on one of our infrequent trips abroad-- would visit a cemetery. He felt that that will show us a lot about the community. And he had a point there because he said, you'll see by the cemetery what kind of people live there, their wealth, because of the monuments, and their kindness, how well they take care of it, of their departed ones. Of course, this was a huge cemetery, and again, in the snow and the winter, and it was a grim and dismal [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] point there.

Shortly thereafter, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Father, like many others, got dysentery and typhoid and rapidly started losing weight. And the doctor prescribed opium against it.

And I remember hunting all over, and there were still some very beautiful pharmacies in the ghetto section. And in one of them they still had the opium, which I was able to get to stop the horrible diarrhea and the loss of weight of my father. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] This was November, maybe, or December of '41. And pretty soon thereafter, the pharmacies had nothing at all because I know that I visited them for some other medications, and nothing was left anymore. And food was getting ever shorter. And we were being fed, as I say, one soup a day and maybe a slice of bread. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

Early November, we were told that another transport was coming from Prague. So my brother and I went down to the railroad siding, where we got off to see who was coming from Prague and who the-- who we might know. And sure enough, close friends, the Khoritzes family, was in this transport. So we briefed them on all the details of what was awaiting them in a, in a nice way, I mean, you know. There was-- once in the ghetto, there was no beating, there was no persecution. There was just hunger and-- and very tight space in which to live.

INT: And did you work?

FHS: No, we did not work at the time yet. And so some friends-- they were the closest friends-- arrived. And they were the ones who, in the summer, in our summer home, would rent from us half the house. And so we were together every summer for many years. And the father played tennis with my mother, and they both were bridge players with my mother. As I say, it was a close summer relationship where we lived in the same house together. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

I remember that I got some infection in the ghetto in my--in my foot. And my father took me to a Viennese doctor to attend to it.

INT: Great.INT: He wanted to make sure that you told certain stories.

FHS: I know, I know.

INT: Do you think that you're hitting those stories? I think you've done a pretty good job. Look right now.

FHS: Well, I should-- I should-- I should look-- look-- look up-- look up now, some things-- there's a--

INT: Are you ready?

CREW: No.

INT: OK.

CREW: Just give me a couple more seconds.

INT: OK.

CREW: Take your time.

FHS: Well, we're getting to the war years now, of course, as you see. So--

INT: Fantastic.

FHS: Well, I tell you something, is for many, many years-- I mean, I wasn't-- I didn't hide it, I-- I--

Soon, hunger was the most preoccupying thought on everybody's mind. And soon, we found out, too, that if you worked, you got a fairly thick soup at the work station a day, in addition to the watery soup that you got maybe in the evening, and the slice of bread. So almost half of your food would come from working. And without it, you could not survive.

So we were eagerly awaiting a-- a relocation from the school and assembly point to individual rooms, or whatever was available in the ghetto, and assignment of labor. After about three months or four months, probably in March of 1942, 10 people were assigned to a room-- maybe seven of us, or something like that, a larger room, unheated, outside of a regular house. It was sort of a add-on in the back of a regular house in-- in Lódz on what was then called in German, [GERMAN] strasse. And it had a leaky roof, and one naked bulb, electrical bulb. The toilets were outside.

And the four of us were assigned there-- three people from Vienna, and a couple from Germany. I might add that at that time, by then, 5,000 Jews from Prague were shipped in, in five transports. 5,000 from Germany, and 5,000 from Vienna.

This, in addition to the 250,000 Jews from Lódz and immediate surroundings, and periodically, the Germans would liquidate another little ghetto in some little village of 500 people, or 1,000, of 200, or whatever, and ship them into the Lódz ghetto.

Of the 5,000 Czech Jews, 257 returned. I often wondered why and how, and only subsequent readings confirmed what I thought happened. We were the-- we did not have any connections. We, of-- of-- in-- in-- in the Lódz ghetto. We didn't know anybody. We were the outsiders.

It was hard to survive for anybody. But people who knew each other-- some people even lived in those particular houses that became part of the ghetto-- had some connections, had some stuff saved, some food set aside, knew where to get a job, get maybe into the kitchen, get to the food distribution and be able to accumulate a little more food.

We were the outsiders. And naturally, while we were not being picked on, we did not get the better jobs. We did not get the additional rations. We did not get to the-- to the trough. And we were getting the scraps and the remnants.

And fewer of us survived. And I wonder whether there are some statistics how many actually survived-- the Viennese transports and the German transports-- probably no more.

In any case, came about March or April, and we moved into this room. And shortly thereafter, my brother got a job, I got a job in some a sheet metal factory. My mother got a job. And we finally started getting a little more food.

With the spring and summer, also more food was, I'm sure, grown in the countryside, and the rations always improved in the spring. You got a little bit of beets, maybe. But I might-- and I would like to emphasize-- throughout the years in the ghetto of Lódz, I did not see an egg, I did not see a piece of butter, I did not see a-- a glass of milk.

The food was very basic. I did not see a spoonful of sugar. We got some beet jam occasionally. We got occasionally a tiny little ration of meat. We had our loaf of bread, I think two pounds of bread per person a week.

And we had our soup, which was sometimes more watery and sometimes less watery, with potatoes and vegetables, and maybe a piece of meat thrown into it. And we would occasionally get some potatoes as a ration. And of course, out of the potato peels, and with the beet jam, we would make a cake.

The interesting vignette-- when you got some beef, it was, of course, the poorest, worst cuts, you had to grind it. So we didn't-- being, I mean, shipped in, we did not have a meat grinder. Well, there were local families who would rent the meat grinder for a small payment, but the one condition was, you could not clean the meat grinder.

So in order to squeeze out the last drop of meat, you ran a potato through it. And-- but you left a little piece of potato in the meat grinder, which was their payment-- to show you how much value-- how valuable that was to them-- the little piece of potato that was stuck on the screen, and on the knife, and in the spool of the meat grinder.

Well, Father-- I remember that, I think my brother had a flu or a cold, and we asked again the Viennese doctor-- and there were quite a few doctors, because it was the intelligentsia, the richer people who were shipped in from Vienna, and from Prague, and from Germany-- came to visit us to see, I think, whether the flu or a cold that either my brother had, or I had. But he looked at my father and said, you are the sicker one.

My father's feet was totally-- were totally swollen. And he said, this is undernourishment. You have to put your feet up. You have to eat a little more.

And I believe that my father started giving part of his rations to us, the boys. And he said, you are the seriously ill. Well, we didn't realize it, we didn't know what it was.

And two or three weeks later, Father died, purely of malnutrition. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] It was June 1st-- 1st of June, of 1942, when my father died. And we walked back to the cemetery, which we visited some six months earlier with him, this time behind the carriers-- his body just wrapped in a sheet, and a little trench dug.

And there were just the three of us, my mother, my brother, and myself. And we just saw his body being dumped into at least the individual grave, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] soil to be thrown back on. That was the end.

We did have some relatives in the ghetto at the time, and held it somewhat against them that they did not participate, so to speak, in the funeral. But by then, everybody was gathering and saving their strength, and to walk-- I don't know, two miles, five miles to the cemetery and back again, required strength that was better-- better saved for one's living.

The relatives that we had was a cousin, who was-- Leo Diamond-- who was an attorney in Prague, and his wife, and their little girl-- two or three-year-old girl. And she had her brother, who was a ophthalmologist, very well known, MD ophthalmologist, with his wife and their two little children.

Being a doctor, he received maybe at that time still some little extra rations, and so they were a little better off, health wise, all of them, than we were. They also lived in a better room, which was better insulated, and which could have-- which could be heated with a little bit of wood that one could get here and there. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

I believe it was spring of '93 when the Germans first--

INT: '43.

FHS: '43-- when they first evacuated all the hospitals, of all the people that were sick. And I wonder whether it was even maybe end of '42. But they evacuated all the sick from the hospitals.

And then, they declared a curfew in the ghetto for several days. And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] everybody had to, on a given hour or day, block by block, assemble outside on the street, where they did their selection in order to reduce the population of the ghetto-- I assume for future shipments of people from the outside towns, again, to build up-- to build it up.

We didn't know exactly what was going on, but I do remember that my mother paired off with a lady from Vienna who was much more gaunt, and I paired up with her son, who was smaller than I was, and my brother who was tall, and quite well, with somebody else.

And indeed, they took the lady from the-- we-- we walked down in pairs. My mother was allowed to stay, while the lady was put-- well, taken away. And so was this friend of mine, with whom I walked from the same room.

And by the time we returned to the room, there were just the three of us. And I don't know whether the German couple-- whether the lady was still surviving. But now, within a short year, perhaps, there were just four of us left from the previous nine, the Viennese husband having died also of hunger, sometime earlier.

This was the only time that there was a selection in the ghetto, itself. Otherwise, people were just allowed to starve and die of hunger. Diseases were not really that prevalent. The Germans were afraid of it.

Chlorine in vast quantities was dumped along the sides of the streets where the sewer would flow, or the water would flow, and things like that. Chlorine was used in abundance to disinfect feces in every-- the-- the-- the outhouses and everything else. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

We continued to work. I, by then, got a job at a fur-making factory. The advantage was that the-- that-- that it was warm. There was a lot of pressing to be done. The steam irons kept the rooms quite warm.

And the expert furriers, whom I was helping, would use little pieces of rabbit fur, and I don't know, cat fur, or what, and stitch those on and make out of it vests for army officers to be used under their uniforms, and I guess on the Russian front.

And I learned-- I was quite interested in how this was done, and how they were stretching the fur, and nailing it on the board so that when it dried it was stretched out, and then added on some more pieces, then cut it out, and sewed it into this-- these vests. And of course, there we got our soups, and we got our rations.

Again, we still believed in carefully rationing our own rations. When we got a loaf of bread for a week. When you got it fresh and hot, you could have eaten the whole thing. So we kept one loaf to age it, to have it a week old. And when it was drier and harder, you chewed on it longer. So we always had one loaf in reserve.

Well, somebody did break into our little room-- they must have known-- and stole that. Maybe we still had a can or two of sardines. Why we kept them for poorer days, I don't know. But we came with some food from Prague in the transport, and we still saved some of it. So that was all stolen away.

Well, I remember this was utterly devastating. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] Actually, this happened after my mother's passed away. So I'll first--

So I went to a friend-- she was a daughter of a close friend of my father. She was very good looking. And she was a girlfriend of the chief of the Jewish police. And they were even planning to perhaps get married.

And he got me then a-- he got me that loaf of bread to replace one loaf of bread. So you could see that he had great power. And he got me also a job in a vegetable distribution center, so I was able to eat raw vegetables-- certainly couldn't bring any home, but I could then pass my ration on to my brother.

Prior to that, in the end of 1942, my mother developed typhoid. And it was a rapidly accelerating typhoid. She had to be hospitalized. And I remember, we were daily going to the hospital. We couldn't enter it, because it was a very-- what do you call it, communicative disease. So we only could see her through the window.

But we passed her some of our food. And mother recovered from typhoid. Unfortunately, they did not clear the hospital of all the sick, and she came home very thin, grossly undernourished, but healthy.

She couldn't stay home too long, because she needed the soup from the workshop. So she went to work into a rag factory where they took the old clothing, and they were tearing it into strips. And I guess then the Germans turned it back into the raw material of cotton, and so forth.

Well, tearing these old rags created a lot of dust. And within two months or so, she developed open tuberculosis and suffered greatly and died in our house.

[DRINKS]

She fought for her life. She, in her high fever, was calling for Prague, [CRYING] and coming home.

[CRYING]

So we were left there, by then, just my brother and I, in this little-- not little, big, but very cold and unpleasant dwelling. The friends, the good friends, the Khoritzes-- that I mentioned earlier-- whom I welcomed as they arrived from Prague, were living up the street in a-- in a normal, basically, a normal room, in a normal house, quite warm and well-insulated and well-kept.

And Mrs. Khoritz, who took good care of the family, and all four of them-- which was already unusual by then-- were still living. That is, her husband, and the twin children, Peter and Kitty, who were my age-- were all living. And she would carefully allocate the food to whoever she felt was-- needed more.

She made a room in one corner of this small room for my brother and me. We moved our bunk bed there and joined them and stayed there from-- after my mother's death, which was two days and a year later after my father, buried in the same cemetery, again, in an individual grave. This time, just the two of us followed the-- the-- the body. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

So we joined them there in summer of '43, and that's where we stayed until I left in March of '44 for some labor camps. And the ghetto was then totally dissolved in the summer of '44, and my brother was shipped to Auschwitz.

So from the death of my mother, and the move to the Khoritzes, life was, again, relatively uneventful. You-- really all you could do was think about food, virtually day and night. You went to work in the morning. The work wasn't hard.

The only Germans that you may have encountered in Lódz was one building occupied by probably half a dozen Gestapo men, who were only interested in the local natives who may have secreted some gold, or diamonds, or whatever, in their houses, or that may have brought them from outside the ghetto when they were moved in.

And I'm sure they had their own informers. And periodically, they would drag somebody in who would never come out. And they tried to extract whatever information, or whatever valuables they may have had, from them. But I never heard of any Czech or German Jew having been brought in, because they already went through all those inspections before they left their own town. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

The ghetto, of course, was the poorest part of Lódz. The better parts were sectioned off for the Gentiles. The street car was cutting through one part of the ghetto. And in order to cross from one side to the other, you had to climb a bridge and go to the other side. As you got weaker, that became an ever greater effort to climb the bridge on one side and walk down the other.

Outside, as I say, are work. You-- you took it easy-- as easy as you could, so that you wouldn't waste much energy. We did read. Some of the Czechs brought along some books, which we were exchanging among us young ones, and-- and we would read them. But that's about all. And you-- you could just dream of a-- a jam, or butter, or-- or a piece of bread, or a potato-- and that-- that was about, as I say, all they could.

There were not too many open spaces, not too many trees. The bark, if there was any bark on the tree, we would fight over it and eat that, or cook it with-- with a coffee grinds-- I mean, there was no coffee grinds, the-- that was-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] what was it, there was a-- a wheat, or-- or wheats that were roasted, and resembled coffee. It was a dark liquid that you brewed.

And the grinds, again, as I say, we would make into cake, if we had a little beet jam, or something like that. Anything and everything was being eaten. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

We brought some beautiful clothing, we brought our watches along, we had flashlights-- you know, things-- nothing had of-- was of great value in the Lódz. There were a couple of stores. And I still remember the owners were 50 pounds overweight, because they were authorized by the Germans to buy a new suit, for example, for one loaf of bread. Later it became only a half a loaf of bread.

And that probably then was traded to the Germans. And they got more food for it, and they gorged themselves on it, seeing all the hunger around them, which I don't blame. And there were only two-- couple of these places where you could do these exchanges.

We were totally isolated. Now, maybe the local people, some of them still had a radio or two. And reading the diaries-- there are a couple of diaries that were found after the war from Lódz-- they-- they-- they were better informed. But we had no idea-- radios, of course, were forbidden. We didn't know any of the natives.

And at one time, there was a rumor, when the Germans were furthest advanced in Russia, there was a rumor that the Russians were at the gate of Warsaw, just to give you an idea of how misinformed we were. We had absolutely no idea of what was going on, on the outside-- no contact with anybody.

There was no smuggling going on like there was in other ghettos. And there was a little bit-- we could send out a postcard a month to Prague, to our relatives. And I don't believe that we got-- no, we did not get any correspondence in return.

I still have those postcards. I turned a couple of them to the Hebrew Union College for their library, which shows the Litzmannstadt ghetto with a Hitler on it. You all know how the ghetto was governed-- that it had the eldest of the Jews, an ex-rabbi Chaim Rumkowski. It had its own fire department. It had own-- its own police.

And it's still, to this day, debatable-- and I discuss it with my son-- of whether the eldest was a collaborator, or whether he was not. It's easy to say that he was a collaborator, and there's no question about it that had it not been for the Jewish help, it would have taken many more Germans to govern the ghetto.

But at the same time, they made the dying easier. You were not persecuted-- you died of hunger. But there was no beating. And as I mentioned, the only Germans were the five Gestapo people-- they did not wear a Jewish star, and they were better fed and better clothed.

And if you happened to meet them on the street, you better quickly stepped off the sidewalk, otherwise they would hit you, and drive you off the sidewalk. Outside of that, as I say, you did not encounter any basic maltreatment.

So here we came to March 1944. And in early 1944, suddenly some food packages started coming to the ghetto. We received our first loaf of bread shipped from Prague, and we received a couple of cans of sardines-- which we were very happy about, because that was the-- was the life saver. And it showed us that with the rations we had, it certainly dramatically improved.

INT: And who sent those?

FHS: Those were sent by the cousin who was remaining in Czechoslovakia-- the only cousin who survived the war. She, Jewish, married a Gentile, like I mentioned before, who was the attorney. And she was able to survive the war.

Had he, as the Gentile husband, had he divorced her, she would have been shipped out the very next day. And I don't know what would have happened to the children, who were little children-- six and 10-- and who were mixed-- of mixed marriage.

Now, he was being pushed and persecuted by the Germans every possible way to divorce her, but he would not, so much so that he was sent to a labor camp. And she continued living in her pre-war apartment. And while she had to wear a Jewish star, she did have still some servant help, who purchased the rations. The kids were allowed to walk on the streets without a Jewish star.

So she survived it well, while her husband suffered in the labor camp the last year of the war. But they survived. They were the only ones, as a matter of fact, of a close family of some 35 or 36 people, who perished.

So in March, 1944, the order came out for the ghetto government to send out-- I forget now, 500 or probably 500 or 1,000 young men for labor in some munition factories. They had to be in-- the healthiest ones. And so the directive went-- went out that those working in the food establishments should report and those over 18.

OK, now, let me go to the bathroom.INT: Feel that the doctors got better treatment and better food.

FHS: Initially, yes.

INT: Is that why you wanted your children to be doctors? Did you want your children to be doctors?

FHS: I wanted my son to be a doctor. I did-- we objected strenuously for my daughter to be one, but she always has been in competition with her, with her older brother. And being a strong feminist, she felt that, you know, she should--

INT: Uh-huh

FHS: --do what she wants to do and so forth. Well, we did stand-- we didn't stand in her way, but, but you know, studying that hard, I'm sure that affected her socially-- socializing.

INT: Uh-huh.

FHS: And she enjoys her profession so much so far, that she's really not that--

March of 1944.

INT: And the reason why you were picked is--

FHS: Yes.

INT: --because you were healthy.

FHS: So--

INT: Wait, wait, wait wait.

CREW: We're rolling. Go.

INT: Oh.

FHS: So I reported to the ghetto authorities as the worker in, in the food establishment, although I did not expect it to be sent out, since, since they stated that you had to be 18. I was still a couple of months short of 18, but the authorities knew that there-- they were not going to have too many volunteers. So they nabbed me the day I reported and put me into sort of a prison for future, when they assemble the full transport, to ship me out.

My brother visited me every day. And we discussed very strenuously whether he should join me, since I couldn't get out, or whether we should split. And it was my and his decision that perhaps we should split since his chances of survival in the ghetto now that we were getting packages of food from Prague were better. And why should he suffer?

And while I was always a little more agile and more scheming, and I felt that he will be better taken care of there, which was true until they liquidated the ghetto, of course, while I was then sent out to a work camp. When they got together, and I believe it was 500 of us, and there were four fellows from Prague with whom I still remain friendly to this day, particularly one, Hannush [? Adler ?] Orblitsky, who lives in Prague.

So we were shipped off to Skarzysko-Kamienna, which is in central Poland, where they had-- where the Germans had vast ammunition factories. Arriving in the camp, there was one Czech or Moravian couple-- this was a, a mixed camp-- who evidently started the camp, and had thus a fairly important position there when they built it couple of years earlier. And he took care of me and helped me out with food, and, and even maybe getting me a somewhat better job in the factory.

I don't remember quite the details. His name was Langer. And I lost contact after we left the camp.

I remember that I had a little infection on my hand which had to be cut open. Of course, in those places, they didn't have any, any anesthetics or deadening. And so it was cut open right there and then and, and drained. And I have a little scar to this day on my left hand, but that was about the only sickness I had.

I know that my little luggage was stolen. I found the empty suitcase, or rucksack, months later. Evidently one of the fellow travelers from Lódz stole it from me. And there you had daily contact in the factory with the Polish Gentiles from the villages. And there, a piece of clothing was extremely valuable.

I-- that's where I sold my Longines watch for quite a bit of food, which I shared with my friend. I had a very nice sports jacket for which I got a lot of food as well. For-- unfortunately, other pieces of clothing disappeared in my luggage.

Well, we were there from about March until July of 1944. And as the Russian Army kept advancing, the order was given to evacuate the factory further west. We helped, which was very heavy labor, and some people got badly injured. We were moving these lathes and milling machines that were made for manufacturing ammunition on pipes. We were rolling them into rail cars, which meant up the incline and pushing and so forth.

And I quickly came to the realization that the heavier angle of leaning to the machine indicated greater effort. And I was always the one leaning lowest, at the lowest angle, and I was never picked on by the Germans, who were beating everybody and anybody to move the machinery faster. We got it all located to Tschenstochau, which is at the eastern edge of Poland, and set up in another buildings. And within a few weeks or so, the factory was once again operating.

And I remember the Christmas of 1944, of course, we were working every day. There were no days off. And the German soldiers guarding us, and rather tearful. And they saw the handwriting on the wall as the Russian armies were now approaching the German border. And one or two of them were kinder. By kinder I mean they didn't push us. I don't remember ever receiving, however, a handout of food or anything from any of the German guards.

Came early January of 1945 and we heard already the artillery outside the city. When the Germans brought in cattle cars to evacuate the inmates, the Jewish inmates, one more time again, the Ukrainian soldiers who were guarding us were trying to get on these cattle cars to be shipped out. And they were being pushed off by the Germans, left there to fight and be captured or killed, while they were saving us for future extermination as, as, as the prisoners from, from Poland.

We were in these open cattle cars without any food, and horrible cold, and wintry weather, and without any water for several days until we arrived in Buchenwald. Fortunately, the four of us Czechs got ourselves a little corner in the cattle car, which gave us a little bit more room and something to lean against. We were packed in.

And we survived it until we, as I say, got out in Buchenwald, which by then, was somewhat under International Red Cross care already, January '45. Definitely we-- as like it was in every camp, the first thing they did, you had to undress. All your possessions were taken away.

You went through a steam shower. And we never knew that there were gas showers as well. That we didn't know, even at that time and at that point yet.

So we got washed, deloused, and given new-- new meaning clothing, which was getting only worse and worse. Prior to it, at least, we had still civilian heavier clothing which we had with some cutouts indicating that we were inmates. Now we got the concentration camp pajamas, the thin pajamas, wooden clogs, and here and there some cotton coats and caps, in January, a very cold winter, of 1945.

Two of my friends stayed in Buchenwald and survived there relatively-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] be more peacefully. My friend Orblitsky and myself were shipped out after three days to a side camp of Dora. And from there to another side camp of Rottleberode, which was an abandoned mill, flour mill, deep in the woods.

Snow was 10 feet high from where we walked at 5 o'clock in the morning, I think two hours to an underground factory where they were manufacturing the rocket-- rockets, V-- V-1s and V-2s, against England. The-- initially, the first two weeks there we were on assignments to cut down trees in the woods for heating the kitchen and heating the soldiers' barrack, while we were freezing cold in, in, in the bunk beds.

Just as a little vignette. I didn't, didn't know-- I knew somewhat about homosexuals. And there was a German supervisor inmate, a German homosexual, who took fancy to me and wanted me all the time. And yeah, fed me good food and wanted me to sleep alone. Well, it was so bitter cold, that we were bundled one against the other. And it was one body's warmth that warmed the other.

And no way would I sleep even for a half an hour somewhere alone. As I said, I didn't quite understand what it was all about. And when it was explained to me that, you know, sexual advances, I told him, look, the war is going to be over shortly. You can have all the women you want, not realizing that that's what he was not-- he was not interested in.

But I mean, he was nice. He never forced any issue. After a couple of weeks, when he saw he wasn't getting anywhere, he no longer fed me any, any food. And that, that ended that.

In the-- in the factory, having a German name, the Germans were already-- and speaking German, I was selected for office work. I was the first Jew in an office. There were some inmates, but those were political prisoners. There was a Czech minister, ex-minister from pre-war days, and some high, high French politicians and things like that. And here I was the first Jew selected for some office filing or what.

And the Gestapo was opposed to it, but the German supervisor there said, I selected him because he speaks a few languages and this and that. And I was able to get Orblitsky into the warehouse. So basically, the work was quite pleasant, and it was quite warm, and we were reasonably well fed. The walk was long back and forth in the heavy snow, because there could be no activity during daytime because the, the planes, Allied planes were, of course, searching for anything to bomb.

Came April of 1945 and the war was coming to an end. And the camp was split into two parts to be evacuated. And the death march started. We were put on a train with open carriages. The train pulled out. Half an hour, an hour later, the planes shot up the, the, the engine. And they also machine-gunned the train.

And a bullet ripped through my coat on the shoulder and hit a Russian prisoner next to me right into the head, and the brain spattered all over me. We jumped out of the carriages. I twisted my ankle somewhat.

They gathered us, and from there on we marched. We marched all day through a heavy woods. And whoever fell by the wayside was shot and killed. With the help of my friend and others, I was able to hobble along until the evening, we came to a pasture. Well, they put us into a cow pasture with a couple of wooden poles surrounding it. And suddenly all the guards disappeared.

Well, my friend said, let's escape. Initially, I was so tired and my ankle was so hurting that I wasn't too keen. But then I said, OK, fine. But if we do escape, I don't want to go into this wood next to the pasture, because it appears to be pretty small. Let's go all the way back to the deep woods that we marched through all day long.

So we did. Escape was very easy. There were no guards. We got out. We marched back another hour or so in the darkness into the woods. We got deeper into the woods.

And there we found some German Air Force uniforms. So we changed into these German Air Force uniforms. There was some toothpaste which we ate. We have not eaten now for two or three days and slept there on the ground.

Next day in the morning, we headed towards a village. I spoke German. We went to the first farmhouse and I explained to them, thinking that my accent probably wasn't the best, that we are from the Sudetenland. They really thought that we were German soldiers, because by then, I guess, even the youngest and the skinniest were being drafted.

The uniforms didn't fit us too well, but I guess it was the end of the war and anything was possible. They fed us everything they had-- salamis and meats and so forth. We stayed only a short while, Went another 10 houses, told them same story, asked them were the German Army was.

And they told us, you'll come to the center of the village. If you'll go to the right, you'll still reach the retreating German Army. To the left are already the Americans advancing. So now the biggest dilemma-- we came to the center of the village with all the villagers watching us, and many of us had fed us and gave us some food, and we turned left. And they were shouting to us, not that way. And I said, we'll fight our way through.

Well, they knew that we were giving ourselves up, they thought. And they didn't mind. And about 15 minutes outside the village, we met the first American tank. And I spoke some English at that time, which came fairly quickly back to me. The Americans didn't think for one second that we were Germans, even though we were in Germans' uniform.

They said, you just go back another half an hour down the road. You'll come to a town, and there is a DP camp. And you just report to the displaced person camp, which we did.

It was run by some Poles. We changed our uniforms for some other clothing. Within a day or two, I was asked to become an interpreter for the local military government. And I did ride with him in the Jeep.

We had a, a gallon can of canned peaches by our bed open so we could dip by hand in any time we wanted and eat the peach. And I must say that in spite of eating, and now, after all this year-- years of starvation, outside of a diarrhea, nothing happened to us. And we-- my friend Orblitsky supervised the cooks, the German cooks.

We was housed in a very nice villa. However, there was nothing to loot. We were looking for things, and the Germans cleaned it all out before. And this went on for something like two or three weeks when the unit was called back to be transferred to the Pacific. I mean, the war was over.

I might add that shortly after we joined the military government, and I should have brought those pictures along but they've been publicized, we were shown some pictures taken by the American Army. All our friends in that cow pasture were, were herded into a barn. Those who were found in the little woods-- and they searched the woods-- were brought into the barn.

The barn was set on fire and machine-gunned. And we were the only two, plus one or two others, four out of 500, who survived that death march. And the pictures, as I say, are shown because the bodies were still lying there in the barn when the Americans came across it.

So when, when the military government was being pulled back, I asked them for a letter of recommendation, which I still have to this day. They confiscated a little four-wheel cart. We loaded our luggage, picked up another two Czech Gentile fellows who were there on a forced labor, loaded our luggage, and decided to walk back towards Czechoslovakia.

And we walked on the, on the freeway, a two-lane, four-lane freeway with no traffic at all, here and there a military car. With our little cart, we were walking south. After about 20 miles or so, two days' march, we came to a village.

Every time we came there, we asked for the military government who put us up and took care of us. Our little cart broke down and we needed another one. Well, this was the most valuable thing in those days. There was no gasoline.

This was the only mode of transportation. Well, the, the captain in charge of that particular district confiscated one against great objections of the German farmer, his little cart, gave it to us. We gave them a little certificate that we'll return it when and if, and continued on until we got to a town of [NON-ENGLISH], of where the Czech-- the buses of-- from Czechoslovakia were there to pick up a refugee-- pick up refugees and laborers and everybody who was Czech, to bring them back to Czechoslovakia.

And so in early part of, let's see, was it late, late May, we got to Prague. They took us to Karlsbad, where we boarded a train. And the train took us to Prague. And I headed, of course, to my cousin's apartment.

She was on vacation with the children in their summer home, but her husband, the lawyer-- and he also had his law offices next door. The, the people in the, in the building told me that he is-- he's going to be back. We waited there on the steps.

He took care of us, the two of us. And we went for a few weeks to the summer home and slowly tried to get back into a normal life. I started school.

Since I was the only one who returned, and had nobody else there, the-- her, the cousin's, husband had a sister who was childless, the couple that always took care of the orphaned children in the Gentile part of the family. So they were really very happy to have me, and they became sort of my foster parents.

They were quite poor. I slowly got back some of our properties. I supported them greatly with the money that they didn't have, while they gave me their love. And I had one room there where I stayed and lived till 1948 when the Communists took over. And a few months later, I escaped to the West, because I decided that I didn't want to have, now, exchange one dictatorship for another.

Experiences-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] having been brought up very-- as a secular Jew, not even. You can't say even. I knew that I was a Jew. I experienced some limited antisemitism and-- in the regular school, and from some people. But it was no more, and maybe even somewhat less, than people experienced in the United States.

But not having had any deep foundation in the Jewish religion, I made a vow then that I will not pass this curse onto my children. I saw that the only ones who survived unscathed and in, to me, great luxury was my cousin, who was in the mixed marriage. And her children didn't suffer one iota. They were already 50% Jewish.

And while they could not and would not have been, under the Nazis, allowed any higher education, I mean, they survived the war in great comfort. And I felt that I was going to give up a religion that meant nothing to me. I made a vow that I will marry definitely a non-Jewish person and save the kids from the same persecution that's been hitting us as Jews every 50, 100 years when we go through the history. Whether it was in Spain, whether it was the pogroms in Russia, whether, you know, whatever and wherever, it has followed us ever since the destruction of the temple in Israel. Well, that was easier, easier said than done.

My years from 1945 to '48 were very bitter ones, not only that I was left alone, but I didn't want to be-- I, I didn't want to even admit that I was Jewish. So I would not associate with Jews. I had nothing in common with the Gentiles who survived the war in relative tranquility.

I could not discuss with them my war experiences. I could not-- and as I say, I had nothing in common with them. If anything, they couldn't-- you know, when they started probing into what happened three, three months or six months or nine months ago, I couldn't tell them. And it was a very difficult, very difficult, trying time, not alone-- not only that I, as I say, lost everybody, and walking through the city, it was a city of dead to me.

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

Wherever my friends lived, wherever all my relatives, they were all gone. Now, there were, yes, the, the Khoritzes that I told you, that took us in in Lódz, the female members-- that is, the girl and her mother-- survived. The father and the son did not, nor did my brother who, when they liquidated the Lódz ghetto, they shipped everybody to Auschwitz.

I was fortunate enough to bypass Auschwitz. Virtually everybody went through Auschwitz one way or another, unless they stayed in Terezín or others. I bypassed it and went to the other camps.

But-- so I did not associate with them. People that I've known and the few that survived, I did not want to associate. I wanted to start a new life.

Well, when 1948 came and the Communists took over Czechoslovakia, and a few months later I was able to get out, officially, to study in England-- and of course, I didn't return-- I quickly realized that all my friends that were abroad, that did get out, and my few remaining relatives were Jewish, and that I only felt comfortable among my Jewish friends. This took a certain amount of time, of course, this conversion, until finally, of course, I married a Jewish girl and came back.

INT: How was it you came to America?

FHS: Well, I was in England in 1948. And my uncle, my only surviving relative besides the cousin, who was living in France-- and he was married to a Gentile lady. They didn't have any children. That was the uncle that sort of could have-- he was very well-to-do-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] could have saved us, so to speak, if he had said, stay in Switzerland. And if you do run short of money, I'll take care of it.

He rightly suggested that if you do leave your own country, then the only place to go to would be the United States. So in 1948, I wasn't too keen one way or another, but I applied for a visa. And it took 2 and 1/2 years.

And by then, my job in England was at a dead end. And it was difficult to get another job in England at the time. And the climate and everything else didn't appeal to me.

And I did have the cousin and, and my aunt, my mother's sister, my closest relatives living in New York, who finally, through South America and everything else, who we parted with in 1938 in Switzerland-- we went back and they went further west-- they were urging me all the time, to, to come to the United States. So I decided I was by-- as I say, I was single, and I was earning some money, that I can certainly go and have a look at the United States when I-- when the visa came through. And if I didn't like it, I could always go back to England.

So I came in March of '51 to New York, and saw immediately that, indeed, the opportunities were far greater. And I got a good job. And within a few months, I had my car and had my friends. And all my friends were Jewish. And my relatives were Jewish. And I slowly, gradually shifted back to my roots.

Now, that did not make me any more religious. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] I can't say that I believe in God, having allowed all that. But the children are very Jewish-minded. And they'll continue the tradition. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] So that's a quick summary.

