As the future of the canned beverages advanced, a local Coca-Cola bottling company wanted to acquire the experience, and also always had high opinion of Arthur MacDonald as a salesperson. So they bought out the White Rock operation and made MacDonald first sales vice president, and within six months, president of the local Coca-Cola bottling company.

And I came with him. And within a year, he made me vice president of manufacturing. At the time, there were no Jews employed in the Coca-Cola operations, and I would have never gotten in through the front door, either. But this way through the back door, through the acquisition, and having Arthur MacDonald as my mentor, as I say, I advanced, much to the surprise of everybody and anybody there, to the-- to the position of vice president of manufacturing.

Coca-Cola Bottling Company grew rapidly through acquisitions, and ultimately our franchise territories covered most of the islands of Hawaii, and all the way to mid-America and Kansas and Omaha, and-- and Madison, Wisconsin.

I occupied-- continued in my position. I think I was pretty well thought of. Ultimately, the company was taken over by Northwest Industries, who then in turn, were taken over by Beatrice Foods, and then over-- then dissected and resold by KKR to Perrier, and so forth.

