But they have been ambitious, as I think many first-generation children are. Because for them, it is not too difficult to, so to speak, beat their parents. It's, I think, the subsequent generation that starts sliding, because there is nothing more to win if their parents have reached fairly high levels. So they try to establish their own individuality, and that's where the diversion comes in. So they both were-- both ambitious, good students and have achieved already good successes early in life.

INT: Did you have any suggestions to them about maintaining a Jewish life, or not maintaining a Jewish life?

FHS: No, I really left it open to them. But the history, I think, has influenced them greatly. And they both are very clearly, very openly Jewish, proud of it, and active in Jewish causes, some particularly so in the second generation, and-- and in many charities.

