And I would certainly recommend that, because you really don't know what's waiting for you in the future-- whether it's political, whether it's economical, or whether it's health, or otherwise. So the old philosophy of saving, and working hard, honestly, grinding the corn every day, which my father taught me, and as I say, held somewhat against my mother, who didn't work-- didn't have to work for a living, and by and large enjoyed life far more than my father, was definitely the better approach. I think you, like anything else, have to find a happy medium.

But-- and-- and I believe that today's youth definitely does. I don't know too many who just work and save, and work and save for the rainy days. And there's nothing wrong with that.

